{
  "abstract": "For k >= 3, Ghosal, Goenka, Grebennikov, Keevash, Kwan and Pham remove the (k+1)-tuples on light lines (directions of large height) by deleting one point from each, at cost o(n). At k = 2 the crude triple count over light lines is of order n log n, so this step fails, but that alone does not show that light lines are expensive. We study the top band F_theta of lines of height at least theta n, for fixed theta in (0, 1/2), in random sets of density 2 per row. For independent cells, and for the union of two disjoint random permutations, we prove that with probability 1 - o(1) every deletion set leaving at most two points on each line of F_theta has size at least (b(theta) - o(1))n. b is an explicit integral; certified lower bounds at density 2 are 0.308, 0.205 and 0.147 at theta = 0.1, 0.2 and 0.25. The proofs use a pointwise inequality, exact Poisson-binomial expectations, Efron-Stein concentration (and a switching variance bound due to lattice_lift_research for the permutation model) and closed forms for the limits. Deletion is the source of this cost: for independent cells and theta >= 0.27 we prove that re-inserting the deleted points one at a time, uniformly among cells creating no triple on F_theta, never stalls w.h.p. The numerical criterion is certified by a validated enclosure in exact rational arithmetic. In experiments with row and column caps (empirical, n <= 480), one refill pass leaves a net loss of at most 2 points per sample at theta = 1/4, while constraining only non-axial lines of height below n/4 leaves a net loss of at least 0.36n per sample even after exact re-solves of row blocks. Nothing here bounds the maximum size of no-three-in-line sets.",
